---
// Card body for /notes/three-ways-to-write-a-material. Rendered on its own page and inside every series that uses it.
import Tex from '../components/Tex.astro';
---
  <h2 id="isotropic">What "isotropic" buys you</h2>
  <p>
    A hyperelastic material stores energy <Tex tex={String.raw`\Psi(F)`} /> per unit volume, as a function of the
    deformation gradient <Tex tex={String.raw`F`} />. Two requirements shrink that function a lot. The energy must not
    change if you rotate the deformed body (frame indifference), and for an isotropic material it must not change if you
    rotate the material before deforming it. Write the singular value decomposition
    <Tex tex={String.raw`F=U\,\mathrm{diag}(\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\lambda_3)\,V^{\mathsf T}`} />: the two rotations are exactly
    <Tex tex={String.raw`U`} /> and <Tex tex={String.raw`V`} />, so what is left is a symmetric function of the three
    <em>principal stretches</em>,
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\Psi(F) = \psi(\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\lambda_3),\qquad \psi(\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\lambda_3)=\psi(\lambda_2,\lambda_1,\lambda_3)=\psi(\lambda_1,\lambda_3,\lambda_2).`} />
  <p>
    (I follow the notation of my papers: <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_i`} /> are stretches, and the Lamé parameters are
    written <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_{\text{Lamé}},\mu_{\text{Lamé}}`} /> to keep the two apart.) Everything about an
    isotropic material is a symmetric function of three numbers. The question is only which three numbers you hand it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="three">Three sets of three numbers</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Principal stretches</strong> <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\lambda_3`} />. To allow inverted
    elements, use the <em>signed</em> SVD of Irving, Teran and Fedkiw (SCA 2004): keep <Tex tex="U" /> and <Tex tex="V" />
    proper rotations and let the smallest stretch go negative when <Tex tex={String.raw`\det F<0`} />.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Lower invariants</strong>, built from the polar decomposition <Tex tex={String.raw`F=RS`} /> (Smith, de Goes and
    Kim, 2019; Eq. 16 of the SCA paper):
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`i=\operatorname{tr}S=\lambda_1+\lambda_2+\lambda_3,\qquad ii=\|F\|^2=\lambda_1^2+\lambda_2^2+\lambda_3^2,\qquad iii=J=\det F=\lambda_1\lambda_2\lambda_3.`} />
  <p>
    <strong>Upper invariants</strong>, the classical invariants of the right Cauchy–Green tensor
    <Tex tex={String.raw`C=F^{\mathsf T}F`} />, used for example by Teran et al. (SCA 2005):
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`I_1=\operatorname{tr}C=\textstyle\sum_i\lambda_i^2,\qquad
    I_2=\tfrac12\big[(\operatorname{tr}C)^2-\operatorname{tr}C^2\big]=\lambda_1^2\lambda_2^2+\lambda_2^2\lambda_3^2+\lambda_3^2\lambda_1^2,\qquad
    I_3=\det C=J^2.`} />
  <p>
    "Lower" and "upper" are the labels used in my papers (the SIGGRAPH Asia 2023 related-work section contrasts the
    lower-invariant analysis of Smith et al. with the upper-invariant one of Teran et al.); the literature itself usually
    numbers both sets <Tex tex={String.raw`I_1,I_2,I_3`} />, and many texts also use isochoric versions such as
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\bar I_1=J^{-2/3}I_1`} />. Check which set a paper means before comparing formulas.
  </p>

  <h2 id="same">The same material, as long as nothing flips</h2>
  <p>
    The lower invariants are the sum, the sum of squares and the product of the signed stretches, which determine all three
    elementary symmetric polynomials of the stretches, signs included. The upper invariants are the elementary symmetric
    polynomials of the <em>squared</em> stretches. For an uninverted element (<Tex tex={String.raw`\det F>0`} />) all three
    sets carry the same information, and the same energy can be written in any of them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The difference shows up when an element is turned inside out. A reflection changes the sign of one stretch and the sign
    of <Tex tex={String.raw`\det F`} />, but <Tex tex={String.raw`C=F^{\mathsf T}F`} /> does not change at all. Anything
    written in the upper invariants, including <Tex tex={String.raw`J=\sqrt{I_3}`} />, gives a mirrored element exactly the
    energy of the unmirrored one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="demo">Try it</h2>
  <p>
    The demo is in 2D, where the lower invariants are <Tex tex={String.raw`\operatorname{tr}S`} />,
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\|F\|^2`} /> and <Tex tex={String.raw`\det F`} />, and the upper ones are
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\operatorname{tr}C`} /> and <Tex tex={String.raw`\det C`} />. Set the two stretches and a rotation,
    pick a material, and compare the energy computed from each representation. Then tick <strong>reflect</strong>: the
    letter inside the square turns into its mirror image, and the column computed from the invariants of
    <Tex tex="C" /> does not notice.
  </p>
  <figure class="note-fig mat-demo">
    <canvas id="mat-canvas" width="880" height="440" aria-label="Interactive demo: a square with a letter F deformed by two stretches, a rotation and an optional reflection."></canvas>
    <div class="mat-controls">
      <label><Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_1`} /> <input type="range" id="mat-s1" min="0.3" max="2" step="0.01" value="1.4" /> <span id="mat-s1v"></span></label>
      <label><Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_2`} /> <input type="range" id="mat-s2" min="0.3" max="2" step="0.01" value="0.7" /> <span id="mat-s2v"></span></label>
      <label>rotation <input type="range" id="mat-rot" min="-3.14" max="3.14" step="0.01" value="0.4" /></label>
      <label><input type="checkbox" id="mat-ref" /> reflect</label>
      <label>material <select id="mat-sel">
        <option value="neohookean">Neo-Hookean</option>
        <option value="arap">ARAP</option>
        <option value="stvk">St. Venant–Kirchhoff</option>
      </select></label>
    </div>
    <div class="mat-read" id="mat-read"></div>
    <figcaption>Dashed: the undeformed square. Solid: the deformed one. The table lists the three sets of numbers and the
      energy density computed from each (2D versions of the materials, <Tex tex={String.raw`\mu=\lambda=1`} />).</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    What to look for. With <strong>reflect</strong> off, the three energies agree to round-off for every material. With it
    on, Neo-Hookean written with <Tex tex={String.raw`\log\det F`} /> becomes undefined (the log barrier is doing its job),
    while the version written with <Tex tex={String.raw`\log\sqrt{I_3}`} /> reports a small, finite energy for an element
    that is inside out. ARAP written in <Tex tex="C" /> loses the sign of the flipped stretch and reports the energy of the
    unreflected element. St. Venant–Kirchhoff depends on <Tex tex="C" /> by definition, so all three columns agree, and all
    three are blind: that is a property of the material, not of the representation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-stretches">Why my papers write materials in stretches</h2>
  <p>
    Both of my material papers settled on the stretch form, for reasons that are easy to state and easy to check.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The rest shape sits on a boundary in invariant space.</strong> By Cauchy–Schwarz,
    <Tex tex={String.raw`3\,ii\ge i^2`} />, with equality exactly when all stretches are equal. So the rest shape
    <Tex tex="(i, ii, iii) = (3, 3, 1)" /> lies on the boundary of the set of reachable invariants, and an energy can have a
    <em>non-zero</em> gradient there while the rest shape is still its minimum. The Linear Corotational material
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mu\sum_i(\lambda_i-1)^2+\tfrac{\lambda}{2}(i-3)^2=\mu(ii-2i+3)+\tfrac{\lambda}{2}(i-3)^2`} /> is
    the example in the SCA paper (Eq. 20):
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial i}=-2\mu_{\text{Lamé}},\qquad \frac{\partial\psi}{\partial ii}=\mu_{\text{Lamé}},\qquad \frac{\partial\psi}{\partial iii}=0\qquad\text{at rest.}`} />
  <p>
    In stretches, the gradient at rest is simply zero.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fewer derivatives to manage.</strong> Because <Tex tex={String.raw`\psi`} /> is symmetric in the stretches but not
    in the invariants, the stretch form has 2 distinct second derivatives at rest and 3 distinct third derivatives; the
    invariant form has 6 and 10. That is why the Lamé parameters of any material are two lines in stretches (SCA Eqs. 5–6),
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\lambda_{\text{Lamé}}=\psi_{12},\qquad \mu_{\text{Lamé}}=\tfrac12(\psi_{11}-\psi_{12}),`} />
  <p>
    and a mouthful in lower invariants (SCA Eqs. 17–18, all derivatives at <Tex tex="(3, 3, 1)" />):
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\lambda_{\text{Lamé}}=\partial_{iii}\psi+\partial_{i,i}\psi+4\partial_{ii,ii}\psi+\partial_{iii,iii}\psi+4\partial_{i,ii}\psi+4\partial_{ii,iii}\psi+2\partial_{i,iii}\psi,\qquad
    \mu_{\text{Lamé}}=\partial_{ii}\psi-\tfrac12\partial_{iii}\psi.`} />
  <p>
    I checked both pairs of formulas against each other for Linear Corotational, Neo-Hookean and St. Venant–Kirchhoff,
    written both ways; they agree. (St. Venant–Kirchhoff in lower invariants needs <Tex tex={String.raw`\sum_i\lambda_i^4`} />,
    which is a cubic expression in <Tex tex="i, ii, iii" />: another small sign that the stretches are its natural home.)
    The gap widens at third order, which is where the SCA paper's nonlinearity measures live (see
    <a href="/notes/material-zoo">A Field Guide to Isotropic Materials</a>).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Stretches are what you can measure and design.</strong> When we fitted materials to poking experiments
    (SIGGRAPH Asia 2023, Section 5), we first tried invariants, and it was hard to say what range of the three-dimensional
    invariant space to sample, or what a sensible curve in it looks like. In stretches, the energy directly answers "what
    happens when the material is stretched or compressed by this much," and many materials are <em>separable</em>
    Valanis–Landel functions <Tex tex={String.raw`\sum_i f(\lambda_i)+h(J)`} /> of one-dimensional curves. Our fitted
    material is exactly such a function, with <Tex tex="f" /> a spline.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The price.</strong> Stretches need an SVD per element, and their derivative formulas contain quotients like
    <Tex tex={String.raw`(\psi_1-\psi_2)/(\lambda_1-\lambda_2)`} /> that are 0/0 when two stretches are equal, which
    includes the rest shape. These singularities are removable, and the SIGGRAPH Asia 2023 paper gives a complete
    treatment; that is the subject of <a href="/notes/isotropic-gradient-hessian">a separate note</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="choose">Which one to use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stretches</strong> are the natural home of Ogden and Valanis–Landel models and of any energy you want to design
      one stretch at a time; with the signed SVD they handle inversion. Cost: an SVD and some careful quotients.</li>
    <li><strong>Lower invariants</strong> keep most of the convenience of polynomials
      (<Tex tex={String.raw`\partial\,ii/\partial F=2F`} />, <Tex tex={String.raw`\partial J/\partial F=\operatorname{cof}F`} />)
      and keep the sign of <Tex tex="J" />. Corotational and ARAP energies are linear in <Tex tex={String.raw`\operatorname{tr}S`} />,
      which has no polynomial expression in <Tex tex="C" />.</li>
    <li><strong>Upper invariants</strong> are smooth and SVD-free, the classical choice in continuum mechanics (Mooney–Rivlin,
      the textbook Neo-Hookean), and blind to reflection. Writing <Tex tex="J" /> as <Tex tex={String.raw`\det F`} /> rather
      than <Tex tex={String.raw`\sqrt{I_3}`} /> is a cheap habit that keeps a model aware of inversion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="checklist">A short checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>When reading a paper, find out which <Tex tex={String.raw`I_1,I_2,I_3`} /> it means before comparing formulas.</li>
    <li>If elements can invert, check whether your energy uses <Tex tex={String.raw`\det F`} /> or
      <Tex tex={String.raw`\sqrt{\det C}`} />. Only the first can resist inversion.</li>
    <li>Do not expect the gradient with respect to invariants to vanish at rest; expect it with respect to stretches.</li>
    <li>Check a new implementation by computing the energy in two representations on random uninverted
      <Tex tex="F" />. They should agree to round-off.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="refs">References</h2>
  <ul class="refs">
    <li>H. Chen and J. Barbič. Weak Nonlinearity and Linear Equalization for Isotropic Hyperelastic Materials. <em>Computer Graphics Forum</em> 45(8) (Proc. SCA), 2026. Section 3.2, Eqs. 5–6, 16–20.</li>
    <li>H. Chen, D. Zhao and J. Barbič. Capturing Animation-Ready Isotropic Materials Using Systematic Poking. <em>ACM Trans. on Graphics</em> (SIGGRAPH Asia 2023) 42(6), 2023. Sections 2.4, 5 and 6.</li>
    <li>B. Smith, F. de Goes and T. Kim. Analytic Eigensystems for Isotropic Distortion Energies. <em>ACM Trans. on Graphics</em> 38(1), 2019.</li>
    <li>J. Teran, E. Sifakis, G. Irving and R. Fedkiw. Robust Quasistatic Finite Elements and Flesh Simulation. <em>Symp. on Computer Animation</em>, 181–190, 2005.</li>
    <li>G. Irving, J. Teran and R. Fedkiw. Invertible Finite Elements for Robust Simulation of Large Deformation. <em>Symp. on Computer Animation</em>, 131–140, 2004.</li>
    <li>H. Xu, F. Sin, Y. Zhu and J. Barbič. Nonlinear Material Design Using Principal Stretches. <em>ACM Trans. on Graphics</em> (SIGGRAPH 2015) 34(4), 2015.</li>
  </ul>


<style>
  .mat-demo canvas { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 6px; }
  .mat-controls { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .5em 1.2em; align-items: center; margin-top: .6em; }
  .mat-controls input[type=range] { vertical-align: middle; width: 8em; }
  .mat-controls select { font: inherit; background: var(--bg); color: var(--ink); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 4px; padding: .15em .3em; }
  .mat-read { margin-top: .6em; font-size: .9em; overflow-x: auto; }
  .mat-read table { border-collapse: collapse; }
  .mat-read td, .mat-read th { padding: .2em 1.6em .2em 0; text-align: left; vertical-align: top; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .mat-read th { font-weight: 600; }
  .mat-read .off { color: var(--link); font-weight: 600; }
  .refs { font-size: .9em; }
  .refs li { margin: .35em 0; }
</style>

<script>
  import { rot, mul, quantities, MATERIALS } from '../scripts/three-ways-to-write-a-material.js';
  const cv = document.getElementById('mat-canvas') as HTMLCanvasElement;
  const ctx = cv.getContext('2d')!;
  const W = cv.width, H = cv.height, SC = 95, CX = W / 2, CY = H / 2;
  const $ = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id) as HTMLInputElement;
  const css = (v: string) => getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue(v).trim() || '#000';
  // the letter F as polylines in reference coordinates (square is [-1, 1]^2)
  const GLYPH = [[[-0.35, -0.6], [-0.35, 0.6], [0.4, 0.6]], [[-0.35, 0.05], [0.25, 0.05]]];
  const SQUARE = [[-1, -1], [1, -1], [1, 1], [-1, 1]];

  function currentF() {
    const s1 = +$('mat-s1').value, s2 = +$('mat-s2').value, th = +$('mat-rot').value, ref = $('mat-ref').checked;
    return mul(rot(th), [s1, 0, 0, ref ? -s2 : s2]);
  }
  const map = (F: number[], p: number[]) => [CX + SC * (F[0] * p[0] + F[1] * p[1]), CY - SC * (F[2] * p[0] + F[3] * p[1])];

  function poly(pts: number[][], close: boolean) {
    ctx.beginPath(); pts.forEach((p, i) => (i ? ctx.lineTo(p[0], p[1]) : ctx.moveTo(p[0], p[1]))); if (close) ctx.closePath();
  }

  function fmt(x: number) { return Number.isFinite(x) ? x.toFixed(4) : 'undefined'; }

  function draw() {
    const ink = css('--ink'), muted = css('--muted'), link = css('--link'), line = css('--line'), bg = css('--bg');
    ctx.fillStyle = bg; ctx.fillRect(0, 0, W, H);
    const I = [1, 0, 0, 1], F = currentF();
    ctx.setLineDash([5, 5]); ctx.strokeStyle = muted; ctx.lineWidth = 1.2; poly(SQUARE.map((p) => map(I, p)), true); ctx.stroke(); ctx.setLineDash([]);
    ctx.globalAlpha = 0.12; ctx.fillStyle = link; poly(SQUARE.map((p) => map(F, p)), true); ctx.fill(); ctx.globalAlpha = 1;
    ctx.strokeStyle = ink; ctx.lineWidth = 2; poly(SQUARE.map((p) => map(F, p)), true); ctx.stroke();
    ctx.strokeStyle = link; ctx.lineWidth = 4; ctx.lineCap = 'round'; GLYPH.forEach((g) => { poly(g.map((p) => map(F, p)), false); ctx.stroke(); });
    ctx.strokeStyle = line; ctx.lineWidth = 1; ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(0, CY); ctx.lineTo(W, CY); ctx.moveTo(CX, 0); ctx.lineTo(CX, H); ctx.stroke();
    $('mat-s1v').textContent = (+$('mat-s1').value).toFixed(2); $('mat-s2v').textContent = (+$('mat-s2').value).toFixed(2);
    const q = quantities(F), m = (MATERIALS as any)[$('mat-sel').value];
    const e = [m.stretch(q), m.invS(q), m.invC(q)];
    const same = (a: number, b: number) => (Number.isFinite(a) && Number.isFinite(b) ? Math.abs(a - b) < 1e-9 : Number.isFinite(a) === Number.isFinite(b));
    const cls = (x: number) => (same(x, e[0]) ? '' : ' class="off"');
    (document.getElementById('mat-read') as HTMLElement).innerHTML =
      `<table><tr><th>stretches</th><th>lower: tr S, ‖F‖², det F</th><th>upper: invariants of C</th></tr>` +
      `<tr><td>λ₁ = ${q.s1.toFixed(3)}, λ₂ = ${q.s2.toFixed(3)}</td>` +
      `<td>tr S = ${q.trS.toFixed(3)}, ‖F‖² = ${q.normF2.toFixed(3)}, det F = ${q.J.toFixed(3)}</td>` +
      `<td>tr C = ${q.I1.toFixed(3)}, det C = ${q.I2.toFixed(3)}</td></tr>` +
      `<tr><td>Ψ = ${fmt(e[0])}</td><td${cls(e[1])}>Ψ = ${fmt(e[1])}</td><td${cls(e[2])}>Ψ = ${fmt(e[2])}</td></tr></table>` +
      `<span class="muted">${m.name}. ${same(e[0], e[1]) && same(e[0], e[2]) ? 'All three agree.' : 'Highlighted: disagrees with the stretch-based value.'}</span>`;
  }

  ['mat-s1', 'mat-s2', 'mat-rot', 'mat-ref', 'mat-sel'].forEach((id) => { $(id).addEventListener('input', draw); $(id).addEventListener('change', draw); });
  matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').addEventListener('change', draw);
  draw();
</script>
